$, i.e., $pq = n$.\n\nWe are given that $m < 20$, so $p + q < 20$. We need to find all possible values of $n = pq$ under this constraint.\n\nLet's list the prime numbers less than 20: $2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19$. We will consider all pairs $(p, q)$ such that $p + q < 20$ and calculate the corresponding $n = pq$.\n\n- If $p = 2$:\n  - $q = 2 \\implies n = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4$\n  - $q = 3 \\implies n = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6$\n  - $q = 5 \\implies n = 2 \\cdot 5 = 10$\n  - $q = 7 \\implies n = 2 \\cdot 7 = 14$\n  - $q = 11 \\implies n = 2 \\cdot 11 = 22$ (not valid since $2 + 11 = 13 < 20$ but $22$ is not in the range)\n  - $q = 13 \\implies n = 2 \\cdot 13 = 26$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 17 \\implies n = 2 \\cdot 17 = 34$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 19 \\implies n = 2 \\cdot 19 = 38$ (not valid)\n\n- If $p = 3$:\n  - $q = 3 \\implies n = 3 \\cdot 3 = 9$\n  - $q = 5 \\implies n = 3 \\cdot 5 = 15$\n  - $q = 7 \\implies n = 3 \\cdot 7 = 21$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 11 \\implies n = 3 \\cdot 11 = 33$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 13 \\implies n = 3 \\cdot 13 = 39$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 17 \\implies n = 3 \\cdot 17 = 51$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 19 \\implies n = 3 \\cdot 19 = 57$ (not valid)\n\n- If $p = 5$:\n  - $q = 5 \\implies n = 5 \\cdot 5 = 25$\n  - $q = 7 \\implies n = 5 \\cdot 7 = 35$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 11 \\implies n = 5 \\cdot 11 = 55$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 13 \\implies n = 5 \\cdot 13 = 65$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 17 \\implies n = 5 \\cdot 17 = 85$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 19 \\implies n = 5 \\cdot 19 = 95$ (not valid)\n\n- If $p = 7$:\n  - $q = 7 \\implies n = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49$\n  - $q = 11 \\implies n = 7 \\cdot 11 = 77$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 13 \\implies n = 7 \\cdot 13 = 91$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 17 \\implies n = 7 \\cdot 17 = 119$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 19 \\implies n = 7 \\cdot 19 = 133$ (not valid)\n\n- If $p = 11$:\n  - $q = 11 \\implies n = 11 \\cdot 11 = 121$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 13 \\implies n = 11 \\cdot 13 = 143$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 17 \\implies n = 11 \\cdot 17 = 187$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 19 \\implies n = 11 \\cdot 19 = 209$ (not valid)\n\n- If $p = 13$:\n  - $q = 13 \\implies n = 13 \\cdot 13 = 169$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 17 \\implies n = 13 \\cdot 17 = 221$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 19 \\implies n = 13 \\cdot 19 = 247$ (not valid)\n\n- If $p = 17$:\n  - $q = 17 \\implies n = 17 \\cdot 17 = 289$ (not valid)\n  - $q = 19 \\implies n = 17 \\cdot 19 = 323$ (not valid)\n\n- If $p = 19$:\n  - $q = 19 \\implies n = 19 \\cdot 19 = 361$ (not valid)\n\nThe valid values of $n$ are $4, 6, 9, 10, 14, 15, 25, 49$. Therefore, there are 8 possible values of $n$.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{8}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{8}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
